<header><p class="st-kicker">duchy · Italy</p><h1 class="st-title">Duchy of Naples</h1><p class="st-meta">661 CE – 1137 CE (approx.) · capital Naples</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Byzantine duchy that became an independent city-state, resisting Lombards and Arabs for nearly five centuries until the Normans took it in 1137.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> remained in Byzantine hands after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> conquest. In 661 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constans-ii">Emperor Constans II</a> appointed Basil as its first duke, subject to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strategos">strategos</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>. Over the next century the dukes came from local families, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stephen-ii-of-naples">Duke Stephen II</a>, who also became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>, ruled with little reference to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> was a Greek-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> city of merchants and landowners, governed by dukes who also controlled <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/cumae">Cumae</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sorrento">Sorrento</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/republic-of-amalfi">Amalfi</a> for a time. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sergius-i-of-naples">Sergius I</a> founded a hereditary dynasty in 840. The duchy fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombards</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benevento">Benevento</a>, but sometimes allied with Arab raiders, earning papal condemnation. Greek liturgy and learning survived alongside Latin, and Naples kept a strong civic identity.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The Normans conquered the surrounding lands in the 11th and 12th centuries. After resisting a long siege in 1135–37, Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sergius-vii-of-naples">Sergius VII</a> made peace with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roger-ii-of-sicily">Roger II</a> and was killed fighting for him at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-rignano">Rignano</a> in 1137; after a brief interlude of self-rule, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> submitted to Roger in 1139 and joined the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-sicily">Kingdom of Sicily</a>. It later became capital of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-naples">Kingdom of Naples</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/duchy-of-naples.jpg" alt="Duchy of Naples" width="320" height="363"><figcaption>Image: MapMaster · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Southern_Italy_1112.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>661 CE – 1137 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>duchy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Naples</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Stephen II, Sergius I, Sergius VII</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Chalcedonian Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Latin, Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Ducatus Neapolitanus</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=899&amp;focus=duchy-of-naples" aria-label="Duchy of Naples on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5029 720 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Duchy of Naples lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5349 976L5709 970M5349 976L5349 965M5349 976L5333 1064M5349 976L5331 963M5349 976L5352 982M5349 976L5358 982M5349 976L5348 971"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5709 970h0M5349 965h0M5333 1064h0M5331 963h0M5352 982h0M5358 982h0M5348 971h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5349 976h0"/><path class="map-dot-me